[00:31.57]Conversation One
[00:33.48]M: It's really cold these days. 
[00:35.16]I'm afraid if the heating in my room can't be fixed soon, I might get frostbite.
[00:41.13]W: What is frostbite?
[00:42.35]M: Frostbite happens when the skin and the tissue under the skin freeze.
[00:46.81]W: What does frostbite look like?
[00:48.69]M: There are different degrees. 
[00:50.43]The first stage of frostbite is often called ¡°frostnip¡± and begins with redness and a pins-and-needles feeling. 
[00:58.18]Getting out of the cold quickly at this time can avoid further damage to the skin. 
[01:03.21]Superficial frostbite is the second stage. 
[01:06.08]It begins when your skin begins to turn pale white or grayish-yellow instead of red. 
[01:11.48]It might even begin to appear blue. 
[01:14.20]Crystals of ice are beginning to form in the skin.
[01:17.34]W: Sounds terrible.
[01:18.60]M: Deep frostbite is the next stage, in which the skin can appear blue and numb to pain and cold.  
[01:25.60]At this point you are in danger of blood clots, and at this stage medical attention is crucial.
[01:31.50]W: That's terrible. How can we prevent frostbite?
[01:34.23]M: Well, first, let's see what kind of people are more likely to get frostbite, so that we'll know how to protect ourselves. 
[01:41.80]People are more susceptible to frostbite if they smoke or have poor blood supply to the legs. 
[01:48.07]Older people and people who live outside without proper clothing, heating and food are also at high risk, as are hikers and hunters who aren't properly clothed and stay outdoors too long. 
[02:01.86]The US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention also warns that anyone who drinks excess alcohol or uses illegal drugs is at high risk. 
[02:11.35]While you may feel like your body is warmer, your core temperature and blood pressure actually drop. 
[02:17.28]And of course, you are more likely to get frostbite if you aren't dressed properly for the weather.
[02:22.67]W: I see. Wish you can have your heating fixed soon, otherwise frostbite is not the only danger, and you might also get a cold.
[02:30.08]M: That's true.
[02:31.68]Questions 1 to 4 are based on the conversation you have just heard.
[02:35.70]1. What's the man's problem?
[02:51.97]2. What is one of the symptoms of first-stage frostbite?
[03:08.85]3. What danger could people be in at the deep frostbite stage?
[03:26.92]4. Who are at high risk of frostbite according to the US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention?
[03:47.58]Conversation Two
[03:49.40]W: Come in, please, Mr. Samuel. 
[03:51.15]Thank you for showing interest in joining us and being a quality manager. 
[03:54.73]So, first, what do you think is the role of a quality manager?
[03:58.46]M: Well, a quality manager, I think, takes much responsibility. 
[04:02.46]Some main roles are: improve existing quality standards and product development processes, regularly review products, systems, processes, and services to check for room for improvements, focus on customer requirements, etc.
[04:17.74]W: You just mentioned customer requirements. 
[04:19.77]What is your approach to handling customer complaints related to quality?
[04:23.89]M: I believe in an active approach where my priority is to listen carefully and empathize with customers' concerns. 
[04:30.85]Next, I believe in collaboration with cross-functional teams to understand the root cause of the problem. 
[04:36.86]I would instantly filter out possible measures to remove or reduce the problem. 
[04:41.68]Also, I prefer maintaining transparency with customers for continuous improvement.
[04:47.39]W: What role does the leader play in quality management?
[04:50.48]M: The leader is responsible for ensuring employees understand the importance of quality and adhere strictly to the most efficient delivery. 
[04:59.11]The leader also encourages the team's feedback and respects the same by implementing feasible ones.
[05:05.04]W: How do you stay updated with the latest quality management trends and techniques?
[05:09.36]M: By learning from genuine websites, attending conferences and seminars.
[05:13.60]And I apply new tools and methods in my daily tasks.
[05:17.12]W: Now, what are your salary expectations?
[05:19.45]M: Well, I move here because my wife got a better job in this city and I knew I may not get a quite high salary as a newcomer in this company. 
[05:27.96]But I don't think I can manage with a salary under 120,000 dollars a year.
[05:33.18]W: Thank you again for taking this interview. 
[05:35.29]We'll inform you of our final decision within a week.
[05:39.14]Questions 5 to 8 are based on the conversation you have just heard.
[05:43.88]5. What position does the man apply for?
[06:01.05]6. What does the man think should be the first step when dealing with customer complaints?
