[07:26.41]Passage One
[07:27.72]La Molina, Spain's oldest ski resort, now faces a threat: a lack of snow. 
[07:33.15]As global temperatures rise, the resort, like many around the world, is being forced to rely increasingly on artificial snow. 
[07:40.19]But fake snow comes at a cost. 
[07:42.15]It is both water and energy intensive¡ªa difficult combination anywhere but especially in a country trying hard to deal with a prolonged and severe drought fueled by climate change.
[07:52.98]That's why La Molina will spend the next three years testing a new snowmaking technique that promises to be far less resource intensive, as well as being able to produce snow at warmer temperatures. 
[08:04.06]The project called the Snow Laboratory will make fake snow by adding a mineral to the water going into snow guns, the machines that pump out water and air at high pressure to create snow.
[08:14.85]The idea is to mimic processes that happen in the clouds. 
[08:18.13]Ice is formed in the atmosphere from water droplets in clouds by a process called ¡°ice nucleation.¡± 
[08:23.69]Pure water droplets can remain unfrozen in clouds at temperatures as low as minus 38 degrees Celsius. 
[08:30.07]But ice nucleation can happen at much higher temperatures when the water droplets interact with particles in the atmosphere.
[08:36.91]Spain has been struggling with strong heat waves and a years-long drought. 
[08:40.88]La Molina is not the only ski resort trying to figure out a future in a warmer, dryer world. 
[08:45.80]This new technology can be a good solution to the problem.
